# Satellite Explorer

A minimal, visualization-first web app for exploring satellites and orbital characteristics.

## Core experience
- Interactive 3D Earth/satellite view
- Altitude and inclination charts
- Satellite details and selection
- Altitude/inclination filters
- Multi-satellite comparison

Optimize for accuracy, clarity, responsiveness, and visual quality. Avoid scope expansion that does not improve exploration.

## Product priorities
1. Data correctness
2. Useful visualization
3. Consistent selection/filtering
4. Usability and visual polish
5. Performance/accessibility
6. Additional features

## Design
Use a minimal, modern, scientific interface: neutral backgrounds, restrained accents, clear hierarchy, consistent spacing, readable charts, explicit units, useful tooltips, and subtle transitions.

Avoid excessive gradients/glow, glassmorphism, decorative cards, animation, redundant statistics/headings, arbitrary colors, and cramped charts. Keep Earth visually subordinate to the data.

Use one consistent visual identity per satellite across globe, charts, and comparison. Do not rely on color alone. On narrow screens, stack/simplify panels rather than shrinking charts until unreadable.

## Data model and integrity
Use the project's existing data/API contract; see `data.md` for field semantics and limitations. Normalize source data into a shared internal representation containing only supported fields (e.g. stable ID, name, orbital properties, position/timestamp when available, source/quality metadata).

Missing values are not zero. Do not plot invalid values. Mark unavailable/stale/approximate data clearly. Keep API/data access separate from visualization logic.

## 3D view
- Clear markers and intuitive camera controls; provide reset.
- Selection must be easy and synchronized with other views.
- Render orbital paths only when supported by the available data.
- Distinguish paths, trails, and historical trajectories.
- Never infer a precise current position from altitude/inclination alone or present arbitrary circles as real orbits.

## Charts
Charts are primary features. Each needs a meaningful title, labeled axes, units, honest scales, useful tooltips, selection states, and missing/filtered-data behavior.

Use kilometers for altitude and degrees for inclination unless the existing app specifies otherwise. State what altitude represents. Prefer a histogram for distributions and scatter plots for relationships; avoid chart clutter.

## Shared interaction state
Maintain one source of truth for:
- selected satellite
- compared satellites
- altitude/inclination filters
- dataset/loading status

Selection must update globe, charts, and details consistently. Filters must affect all relevant views using the same logic, show active values/counts, reset cleanly, and handle empty/missing results. Define behavior when filtering excludes the selected satellite.

Comparison must use stable IDs, comparable values/units, consistent identities, and sensible limits when needed.

## Quality
Keep interaction responsive. Avoid redundant renders/requests; separate expensive calculations; reuse normalized/derived data; optimize for realistic dataset size without premature complexity.

Provide accessible control names, keyboard/focus support where practical, readable contrast, reduced-motion support, and a non-3D route to inspect satellites. Independent panel failures should not block the full dashboard.

## Roadmap
1. Understand existing code and data flow
2. Establish visual foundation
3. 3D orbital view
4. Altitude/inclination charts
5. Shared filtering/selection
6. Multi-satellite comparison
7. Performance, accessibility, error handling, polish

Implement incrementally; each milestone should leave the app working. If data/API limitations block a feature, use the best supported alternative and state the limitation rather than fabricating data.

Operational coding rules and verification workflow are defined in `AGENTS.md`.